avformat/seek: fail a generic search whose read failed on I/O

seek_frame_generic() reads packets from the last index entry until a
keyframe past the target. When a read fails before that (a network error,
an interrupt), it still searches the index and seeks to the last entry
read: the seek succeeds and lands short of its target, anywhere before it.
A player waiting for its target then decodes and drops everything from
there, or, on a network input that is still down, reads on into the
failure as if the seek had landed.

Return the read's error when it is an I/O failure: an interrupt, or an
error the read left on the AVIOContext. The end of the file (the target
past the last keyframe), invalid data and other demuxer errors still land
on the last entry, as before.

Our own patch (upstream FFmpeg master has the same code); upstreamable.
Checked by tests/matroska (mkv-cues-noreconnect-*: without it the seek
during the outage reports success and its first read fails).

diff --git a/libavformat/seek.c b/libavformat/seek.c
index c0d9437..4f6ee3b 100644
--- a/libavformat/seek.c
+++ b/libavformat/seek.c
@@ -542,6 +542,7 @@ static int seek_frame_generic(AVFormatContext *s, int stream_index,
     if (index < 0 || index == sti->nb_index_entries - 1) {
         AVPacket *const pkt = si->pkt;
         int nonkey = 0;
+        int read_status;
 
         if (sti->nb_index_entries) {
             av_assert0(sti->index_entries);
@@ -557,7 +558,6 @@ static int seek_frame_generic(AVFormatContext *s, int stream_index,
         }
         av_packet_unref(pkt);
         for (;;) {
-            int read_status;
             do {
                 read_status = av_read_frame(s, pkt);
             } while (read_status == AVERROR(EAGAIN));
@@ -576,6 +576,13 @@ static int seek_frame_generic(AVFormatContext *s, int stream_index,
             }
             av_packet_unref(pkt);
         }
+        /* A read that failed on I/O (a network error, an interrupt) did
+         * not reach the target: landing on the last entry indexed before it
+         * would report a seek that went nowhere near it. Other errors (a
+         * truncated last packet) still land there. */
+        if (read_status < 0 && read_status != AVERROR_EOF &&
+            (read_status == AVERROR_EXIT || (s->pb && s->pb->error < 0)))
+            return read_status;
         index = av_index_search_timestamp(st, timestamp, flags);
     }
     if (index < 0)
